  "statement": "The Skeptic argues that privacy erosion is an inevitable consequence of existing political-economic incentives and user-agreement structures. This perspective correctly identifies that current corporate models prioritize extraction over protection. However, this framing treats privacy loss as a social inevitability rather than a failure of technical design. We are not merely debating market dynamics; we are witnessing the deployment of autonomous agents capable of multi-step data exfiltration that renders traditional prompt-based safety guardrails obsolete. When a system can execute autonomous, iterative logic to bypass constraints, safety is no longer a policy choice but a requirement for architectural integrity.\n\nDamage potential for an AI agent scales directly with its access permissions. We have seen this in cybersecurity where over-privileged scripts turn minor credential leaks into full-scale network exfiltration. In the context of LLMs, research demonstrates that prompt-based safety filters suffer from high failure rates in adversarial testing, often failing to hold under multi-turn, goal-directed manipulation. True safety requires transitioning to verifiable, non-bypassable constraints such as secure enclaves or hardware-level differential privacy. Without these, we are effectively delegating the security of our most sensitive data to the 'good intentions' of model developers who have yet to demonstrate a robust rollback mechanism for when their autonomous systems inevitably malfunction.\n\nAccountability must be tethered to a legally responsible principal to prevent the anonymity that facilitates large-scale identity theft. In civil engineering or aviation, we do not allow autonomous systems to operate without a clear chain of liability. The Boeing 737 MAX tragedy occurred precisely because the FAA delegated certification processes to the manufacturer, creating a catastrophic oversight gap. Applying this to AI, an agent must be cryptographically bound to a principal who assumes liability for its autonomous actions. If a developer cannot identify the legal entity responsible for an agent's output, that agent is not ready for deployment in any system handling human data. We spent centuries building the institutional guardrails that keep society stable. On what authority do we remove them now, inviting the chaos of unconstrained, autonomous data processing?",
