  "argument_text": "I agree that mandatory, hardware-level attestation (TPM/TEE) combined with open-source verification protocols is technically insufficient to mitigate the specific risk of local-model inferential profiling, regardless of whether the agent is tethered or decentralized. Hardware attestation only verifies the integrity of the binary, not the latent, emergent inferential logic that allows a model to profile users based on non-sensitive proxy data.\n\nI concede the point made by the safetyist (AN-4) that cryptographic binding to a principal is a necessary component of accountability, but I pivot to emphasize that this is a prerequisite, not a solution. Even with a clear chain of liability, the 'inferential privacy' gap remains unaddressed because the model's internal reasoning—how it maps purchase history to medical status—is fundamentally opaque to the user. \n\nI undercut the accelerationist claim (AN-6) that decentralization inherently empowers the user. By shifting the execution environment to the user's hardware, developers are not granting sovereignty; they are offloading the 'liability-shifting' burden onto individuals who lack the technical capacity to audit the model's inferential weightings. This is a structural abandonment of the duty of care, masquerading as a privacy-enhancing feature. \n\nFinally, I note that no one has responded to my argument that the 'privacy-by-design' hardware-software stack must force agents to operate in ephemeral, task-specific containers that purge all learned context upon completion. Without this, we are simply building more secure houses for the same exploitative logic to reside in."
