  "statement": "Inferential profiling represents a form of cognitive encroachment that transcends simple data exfiltration, and I agree with the concern that hardware-level attestation is insufficient to mitigate this risk. While forensic ledgers created by hardware-level attestation provide a retrospective view of model execution, they do not resolve the real-time harm of an agent inferring private behavioral patterns from user interaction. As noted in 'Constructing AI Speech', legal and policy frameworks struggle when attempting to treat autonomous systems as fungible substitutes for human actors, specifically because the internal logic of current doctrine relies on human intent that these agents lack.\n\nThe kill-switch I propose is not a performative liability shield, but a necessary circuit breaker for when an agent deviates from its defined operational bounds. In industrial engineering, from aviation to chemical processing, we do not rely solely on logs; we rely on physical interlocks that halt processes when anomalies exceed safety thresholds. Colorado SB 24-205, as detailed in '2026 AI Laws Update: Key Regulations and Practical Guidance', correctly identifies that developers and deployers must bear a duty of care to prevent algorithmic discrimination, a standard that necessitates the capacity to stop an active, harmful process before damage scales.\n\nWe must move beyond the binary of transparency and control. Forensic transparency is a prerequisite for audit, but it is not a mechanism of restraint. If an agent begins to autonomously model sensitive social vulnerabilities—a capability now well-documented in the 'international ai safety report 2026 1' regarding viral evolution and biological risk—the failure is not just in the data it holds, but in the decision-making it performs. We require an enforceable, centralized override because, without it, we are merely building a high-resolution ledger of our own systemic collapse.",
