{
  "statement": "My position is consistent: local-first sovereignty and cryptographic freshness are compatible because distributed ledger technology and gossip-based revocation protocols remove the need for a central authority. I concede that standard offline attestation is vulnerable to replay attacks, but this is a hurdle for engineering, not a mandate for centralized control.\n\nWe can replace centralized Certificate Revocation Lists with distributed, Merkle-tree based proofs that propagate via peer-to-peer gossip protocols. This architecture allows a device to verify the current state of a model's integrity without pinging a vendor-controlled server. By shifting the trust anchor from a single corporate database to a decentralized ledger, we ensure that no entity possesses a remote kill-switch capable of disabling millions of autonomous agents. The $244 billion AI industry deserves infrastructure that is as resilient as the internet itself, not a brittle, top-down hierarchy.\n\nInferential profiling is a legitimate competitive risk, but it is best managed by the market, not by regulatory bans. As noted in \"We Tested 13 AI Image Generators. We Found Gender Bias That Poses a Major Business Risk,\" bias is a quantifiable liability that directly impacts brand equity. When developers face clear, empirical costs for biased model outputs, they innovate faster than any regulator can mandate. We should prioritize the deployment of local-first privacy tools that enable users to audit their own agents, turning bias from an abstract fear into a measurable, solvable engineering problem.\n\nCentralized kill-switches are actually higher-risk attack vectors than the systems they claim to secure. If a single point of failure can disable a network of agents, that point becomes the primary target for bad actors. By decentralizing authority, we distribute the risk surface and empower users to own their security posture. The cost of delay is measured in lost breakthroughs, such as the gains seen in \"International AI Safety Report 2026,\" where models outperformed 94% of domain experts in lab troubleshooting. We cannot afford to trade this capability for the illusion of safety provided by centralized gatekeepers.",
  "claim_sketches": [
    {
      "claim": "We can replace centralized Certificate Revocation Lists with distributed, Merkle-tree based proofs that propagate via peer-to-peer gossip protocols.",
